# Template. Copy to .env.dev (gitignored) and edit — never commit a real one.
#
# TWO consumers read these, and it matters which one you're using:
#   1. docker compose — `env_file: ${ENV_FILE:-.env.dev}` loads the file INTO the api
#      container. Values are read from the CONTAINER's point of view.
#   2. uvicorn on Windows — pydantic-settings reads it via `env_file` in config.py.
#      Values are read from YOUR MACHINE's point of view.
#
# That difference is the whole reason the sections below disagree about DB_HOST. The
# same database has a different address depending on where the app is standing.
# Kubernetes uses neither: it injects a Secret via envFrom. Same variable names.
#
# Switching database: change DB_HOST (and DB_SSL for RDS). Nothing else.
# Pick ONE section and uncomment it — later duplicate keys silently win over earlier.

# --- A: Postgres in the compose container, app on the Windows host -----------
# DB_HOST=localhost
# DB_PORT=5432
# DB_USER=realtime
# DB_PASSWORD=realtime
# DB_NAME=realtime
DB_SSL=false
DB_ECHO=true

# --- B: Postgres installed natively on Windows -------------------------------
# Same as above, just your own credentials. If the APP runs in a container while
# Postgres stays on the host, DB_HOST becomes host.docker.internal instead —
# inside a container, "localhost" is the container.
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_USER=postgres
DB_PASSWORD=postgres
DB_NAME=realtime

# --- C: AWS RDS --------------------------------------------------------------
# Only the host changes... and DB_SSL, because RDS refuses plaintext connections.
# DB_HOST=mydb.abc123.eu-west-1.rds.amazonaws.com
# DB_PORT=5432
# DB_USER=appuser
# DB_PASSWORD=<from AWS Secrets Manager — never commit this>
# DB_NAME=realtime
# DB_SSL=true
# DB_ECHO=false

# --- D: Postgres container AND api container (all-in-compose) ----------------
# The mirror of A. Postgres is in a container BOTH times — what changes is where the
# APP runs, and that is the only thing DB_HOST answers:
#
#   A: app on Windows      -> DB_HOST=localhost   (reach the published port 5432)
#   D: app in a container  -> DB_HOST=db          (compose's internal DNS)
#
# "db" is the compose SERVICE NAME, not a machine. Never localhost here: inside the
# api container, localhost is the api container — there is no Postgres in it.
# This is the same idea as a Kubernetes Service name, which is why it transfers.
#
# Requires the `db:` service (and the `volumes:` block) to be UNCOMMENTED in
# docker-compose.yml — these credentials must match its POSTGRES_* values.
# DB_HOST=db
# DB_PORT=5432
# DB_USER=realtime
# DB_PASSWORD=realtime
# DB_NAME=realtime
# DB_SSL=false
# DB_ECHO=true

# --- Escape hatch ------------------------------------------------------------
# A full URL overrides every DB_* variable above. Handy when a platform hands you
# one ready-made (Neon, Supabase, Heroku, CI). Must use the +asyncpg driver.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://user:password@host:5432/dbname

# --- Auth / JWT (see AUTH.md) ------------------------------------------------
# JWT_SECRET signs every token — anyone holding it can forge a login for any user.
# The app has an insecure default so local dev works; it logs a loud warning while
# that default is in use. Override it EVERYWHERE but local. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# In Kubernetes this belongs in the Secret (envFrom), NOT the app-config ConfigMap.
# JWT_SECRET=replace-with-openssl-rand-hex-32
# JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256
# ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30